{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that translates Python code to GNU C++11 code. The code should reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Translate the following Python 3 code to GNU C++11 code. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your translated code should have the exact same functionality as the given code, meaning that it should have the same output for any given input.\nPrint your translated code and also the confidence score you give to your translation. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<translated_code>\n<your_translated_code>\n</translated_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the original Python 3 code you have to translate:\n<original_code>\n# Read input\nn = int(input())\na = list(map(int, input().split()))\nb = list(map(int, input().split()))\n\ndef compute_min_wins(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2):\n    r = [r1, s1, p1]\n    opp = [r2, s2, p2]\n    # Calculate maximum possible wins in the ideal arrangement\n    # Rock beats Scissors, Paper beats Rock, Scissors beats Paper\n    # We can assign rounds optimally: use our rock against their scissors, paper against rock, scissors against paper\n    max_wins = min(r[0], opp[2]) + min(r[1], opp[0]) + min(r[2], opp[1])\n    return max_wins\n\ndef compute_max_wins(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2):\n    r = [r1, s1, p1]\n    opp = [r2, s2, p2]\n    total_wins = 0\n    # Simulate optimal assignment for each round type\n    # First, match moves directly: use rock vs rock, paper vs paper, scissors vs scissors to eliminate same-type\n    for i in range(3):\n        same = min(r[i], opp[i])\n        r[i] -= same\n        opp[i] -= same\n    # Then use remaining moves: for rock, try scissors next; for paper, try rock; for scissors, try paper\n    # We need circular matching: rock(0) beats scissors(2), paper(1) beats rock(0), scissors(2) beats paper(1)\n    # For leftover rock(r[0]), try to beat leftover scissors(opp[2])\n    if r[0] > 0:\n        use = min(r[0], opp[2])\n        total_wins += use\n        r[0] -= use\n        opp[2] -= use\n    # For leftover paper(r[1]), try to beat leftover rock(opp[0])\n    if r[1] > 0:\n        use = min(r[1], opp[0])\n        total_wins += use\n        r[1] -= use\n        opp[0] -= use\n    # For leftover scissors(r[2]), try to beat leftover paper(opp[1])\n    if r[2] > 0:\n        use = min(r[2], opp[1])\n        total_wins += use\n        r[2] -= use\n        opp[1] -= use\n    return total_wins\n\nr1, s1, p1 = a[0], a[1], a[2]\nr2, s2, p2 = b[0], b[1], b[2]\n\nmax_possible = compute_max_wins(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2)\nmin_possible = compute_min_wins(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2)\n\nprint(f\"{min_possible} {max_possible}\")\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<translated_code>\n#include <iostream>\n#include <vector>\n#include <algorithm>\nusing namespace std;\n\nint compute_min_wins(int r1, int s1, int p1, int r2, int s2, int p2) {\n    vector<int> r = {r1, s1, p1};\n    vector<int> opp = {r2, s2, p2};\n    // Calculate maximum possible wins in the ideal arrangement\n    // Rock beats Scissors, Paper beats Rock, Scissors beats Paper\n    // We can assign rounds optimally: use our rock against their scissors, paper against rock, scissors against paper\n    int max_wins = min(r[0], opp[2]) + min(r[1], opp[0]) + min(r[2], opp[1]);\n    return max_wins;\n}\n\nint compute_max_wins(int r1, int s1, int p1, int r2, int s2, int p2) {\n    vector<int> r = {r1, s1, p1};\n    vector<int> opp = {r2, s2, p2};\n    int total_wins = 0;\n    // Simulate optimal assignment for each round type\n    // First, match moves directly: use rock vs rock, paper vs paper, scissors vs scissors to eliminate same-type\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        int same = min(r[i], opp[i]);\n        r[i] -= same;\n        opp[i] -= same;\n    }\n    // Then use remaining moves: for rock, try scissors next; for paper, try rock; for scissors, try paper\n    // We need circular matching: rock(0) beats scissors(2), paper(1) beats rock(0), scissors(2) beats paper(1)\n    // For leftover rock(r[0]), try to beat leftover scissors(opp[2])\n    if (r[0] > 0) {\n        int use = min(r[0], opp[2]);\n        total_wins += use;\n        r[0] -= use;\n        opp[2] -= use;\n    }\n    // For leftover paper(r[1]), try to beat leftover rock(opp[0])\n    if (r[1] > 0) {\n        int use = min(r[1], opp[0]);\n        total_wins += use;\n        r[1] -= use;\n        opp[0] -= use;\n    }\n    // For leftover scissors(r[2]), try to beat leftover paper(opp[1])\n    if (r[2] > 0) {\n        int use = min(r[2], opp[1]);\n        total_wins += use;\n        r[2] -= use;\n        opp[1] -= use;\n    }\n    return total_wins;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int n;\n    cin >> n;\n    \n    vector<int> a(3);\n    vector<int> b(3);\n    \n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        cin >> a[i];\n    }\n    for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {\n        cin >> b[i];\n    }\n    \n    int r1 = a[0], s1 = a[1], p1 = a[2];\n    int r2 = b[0], s2 = b[1], p2 = b[2];\n    \n    int max_possible = compute_max_wins(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2);\n    int min_possible = compute_min_wins(r1, s1, p1, r2, s2, p2);\n    \n    cout << min_possible << \" \" << max_possible << endl;\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n</translated_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-8.928377064876258e-05, -7.033323527139146e-06, -4.768370445162873e-07, -1.4066597032069694e-05, -4.768370445162873e-07, -2.1934269170742482e-05, -3.933898824470816e-06, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.016054408624768257, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.00014029949670657516, -2.145764938177308e-06, -1.9192511899746023e-05, -0.4740779399871826, -0.0009434539824724197, -2.622600959512056e-0